# Family history books

This guide helps you set up a family history book, where each significant person get a chapter. That person's record at the top of their chapter identifies when they were born, married and died, and who their parents were. Family relationship panels, trees, timelines and an index of people are populated from those records, and faces in old photographs can be named and linked to each person's chapter. When you add information to that record the rest of the book can make use of it without you manually maintaining each other chapter that references them.

![Ewen Kerr's chapter on a phone: his birth year and birthplace, his parents Thomas and Margaret, each linked to their chapter, and a family photograph from about 1910 whose caption names the people in it, linked to their chapters.](../Images/screen-family-finished.png){.figure .screenshot width=378}

The examples come from a sample book about an invented family, the McInneses of Carlton: a tailor from Inverness-shire, his wife from Glasgow, and their children and grandchildren in Melbourne's inner north. The photographs are of real but unidentified sitters, from the Los Angeles County Museum of Art and the Rijksmuseum. To see every chapter's source, [download the sample book](https://sample.readitinabook.com/McInnes-Family-of-Carlton.epub) and open it with [Open an EPUB…]{.ui} on the [Books]{.ui} screen.

## Set up the book

New books are written in Djot, which this needs. The extensions are in Advanced Mode.

1. Click [Settings]{.ui}, then [General]{.ui}, and tick [Advanced mode]{.ui}.

2. Click [Content transforms]{.ui}. Under [Catalog]{.ui}, the extensions are listed in groups. Click [Add to project]{.ui} for each of these, in this order:

   - [Figures]{.ui}, under [Accessibility features]{.ui}, near the end of the list.
   - [Photo Regions]{.ui}, under [Chapter content generation]{.ui}.
   - [Family History]{.ui}, also under [Chapter content generation]{.ui}.

3. Click [Plugins]{.ui} and tick [Photo Regions]{.ui}. This adds a panel for marking the faces in a photograph. Close the settings.

4. Check the book's language in [Book]{.ui} › [Details]{.ui}. Dates are written in the book's language: `en-AU` and `en-GB` give "12 March 1828", and `en-US` gives "March 12, 1828".

Each extension you add puts a script in the list under [Page transforms]{.ui}, at the top of the [Content transforms]{.ui} page. The scripts run from top to bottom, so check that the three are in this order, and use the arrows to move one that is not:

![The list under Page transforms: transformDom.js, then transformFigures.js from figures, transformRegions.js from photo-regions and transformFamily.js from family-history, each with up, down and remove buttons.](../Images/screen-family-transforms.png){.figure .screenshot width=542}

## Start with two people

Try it first with a husband and wife.

1. Click [Append Item]{.ui}, the plus button beside [Chapters]{.ui}. Click the new chapter's pencil button, change the *ID* to `isabella_ross`, and click [Save]{.ui}.
2. Replace the chapter's text with this:

   ``` djot
   ---
   display: Isabella McInnes
   birth: 1836~
   ---

   # Isabella McInnes

   :family:
   ```

3. Add a second chapter in the same way, with the ID `ewen_mcinnes`, and this text:

   ``` djot
   ---
   display: Ewen McInnes
   birth: 1822-04-02
   partner: isabella_ross
   married: 1858-09-02
   ---

   # Ewen McInnes

   :family:
   ```

4. Click Isabella's chapter again.

Each panel names the other person as partner, with a link to their chapter, although the marriage is written only in Ewen's record.

## Give each person a chapter

Each person's chapter has an id, such as `margaret_mcinnes`: lowercase, with `_` between the words. Other chapters use the id to refer to the person, so choose it once and keep it. Many family historians use a woman's birth surname in her id, so that the id stays the same whether a record calls her by her maiden or her married name. To set a chapter's id, click its pencil button in the chapter list and change the *ID*.

![The chapter list of the sample book: a chapter called about, one called family, one chapter for each person named with their id, such as ewen_mcinnes and isabella_ross, then people for the index and research for the notes.](../Images/screen-family-chapters.png){.figure .screenshot width=240}

Chapters that are not about one person, such as the introduction and the research notes, can have any id.

## Write a record

The record goes at the very top of the chapter, between two lines of three hyphens. Write `:family:` on a line of its own where the person's family should appear.

Margaret McInnes's record gives her parents and her marriage. Her panel lists her parents, her brother and her husband, each linked to their chapter. Her brother is not in her record: he is found because his record names the same parents.

![Margaret McInnes's chapter in the editor, with her record at the top: her name, birth date and birthplace, her parents, her husband and their marriage. Beside it, the preview shows her chapter heading and a family panel listing her dates, her parents, her brother and her husband, with links to their chapters.](../Images/screen-family-panel.png){.figure .screenshot}

Each line of a record follows the same rules:

- A line is a name, a colon, a space, and then the value: `birthplace: Carlton`.
- Put quotation marks around a value that contains a comma or a colon: `married_place: "Lygon Street, Carlton"`.
- Square brackets hold a list, with commas between the items: `parents: [ewen_mcinnes, isabella_ross]`.
- A line that belongs to the line above it starts with two spaces.
- A line that starts with `#` is a comment. Comments never appear in the book, which makes them the place to note where a fact came from, or why you doubt it.

Leave out any line you do not know. The table at the end of this page lists every line a record can have. Never list a person's children or brothers and sisters: they are worked out from everyone's `parents`.

### More than one marriage

John McInnes's first wife died four years after their wedding, and he married again. When a person married more than once, list each marriage under `partner`, in order, each on its own line starting with a hyphen, with its date inside the curly brackets:

``` yaml
partner:
  - { id: mary_doyle, married: 1884-03-12 }
  - { id: emily_barnes, married: 1892 }
```

With a list of partners, leave out the separate `married` line, since it cannot be matched to one of the marriages.

## Write dates

Write dates as year, month and day, joined by hyphens: `1890-04-09`. A month and year (`1890-04`) or a year alone (`1890`) also work.

- For a date you know only roughly, add `~` after it: `1836~` appears as "c. 1836".
- For a date the records give but you doubt, add `?`: `1862?` appears as "1862?".

Ages in records seldom agree. Isabella McInnes was 20 on her marriage certificate in 1858, 42 on her son's birth certificate in 1878 and 79 on her death certificate in 1913, which points to any year from 1834 to 1838. Choose the middle year as an approximate date, `1836~`, and give the ages and their sources in a comment and in your research notes.

## Record people without a chapter

Not everyone needs a chapter. Record the others under `others:` in the chapter they belong with, using the same lines as a record. Neither of John McInnes's wives has a chapter, so both records are in his.

``` yaml
partner:
  - { id: mary_doyle, married: 1884-03-12 }
  - { id: emily_barnes, married: 1892 }
others:
  mary_doyle:
    display: Mary McInnes
    birth: 1863
    death: 1888-07-21
  emily_barnes:
    display: Emily McInnes
    birth: 1865~
    birthplace: Ballarat
```

An id under `others:` works everywhere a chapter's id does, except that the name is not a link.

## When panels and trees catch up

A person's record is read when their chapter is shown in the preview, and the other chapters use the copy saved then. A panel or a tree can therefore leave out someone you have just added, until you open that person's chapter once.

When you package the book, tick [Re-render every chapter first]{.ui} on the [Share]{.ui} page, so that every chapter shows everyone. After adding several people at once, open each new chapter before you package.

## Add a tree, a lifeline or an index

Trees, lifelines, portraits and the index are added with markers. A marker is a word between colons, on a line of its own, such as `:tree:`.

Some markers take settings in curly brackets straight after the marker: `:tree:{of=ewen_mcinnes depth=2}`. Put spaces between the settings, and put quotation marks around a value that contains a space: `as="Uncle Jack"`.

Without `of=`, a marker is about the chapter's own person. With `of=`, it is about the person with that id, so you can put it in any chapter.

### Draw a family tree

`:tree:` draws the person's descendants. `depth=2` shows two generations below them.

![The editor with a heading and one line, a tree marker for Ewen McInnes with a depth of two. The preview shows Ewen and Isabella at the top, their children John, Margaret and Robert below them with John's two wives and Margaret's husband, and each grandchild under their parents.](../Images/screen-family-tree.png){.figure .screenshot}

To show how two relatives are connected, draw the path between them through the ancestor they share: `to=` names one relative and `via=` names the ancestor. Both relatives must be descended from that ancestor. The cousins Catherine McInnes and Ewen Kerr meet at their grandfather, Ewen McInnes.

![The editor with a tree marker from Catherine McInnes to Ewen Kerr, through Ewen McInnes. The preview shows Ewen and Isabella at the top, then two branches: John with his daughter Catherine, and Margaret with her son Ewen.](../Images/screen-family-path.png){.figure .screenshot}

### Compare lives with lifelines

A lifeline shows one person's birth, marriages and death along a line of years. Lifelines written on consecutive lines share one scale, so you can see whose lives overlapped. `as=` gives the label at the start of each line.

![The editor with three lifeline lines, for Ewen McInnes, his wife Isabella and their daughter Margaret. The preview shows three lines of years on one scale: Ewen from 1822 to 1890, Isabella from about 1836 to 1913, and Margaret from 1866, with the marriages marked.](../Images/screen-family-lifelines.png){.figure .screenshot}

### Make an index of people

`:family-index:` lists everyone in the records under their surname, with a link for each person who has a chapter. Put it in a chapter of its own at the back of the book, so that readers can find it from the contents.

![The editor with a heading and the index line. The preview lists the Kerrs, the McInneses and the Rosses by first name, with links for those who have a chapter.](../Images/screen-family-index.png){.figure .screenshot}

## Name the faces in a photograph

First add the photograph to the chapter, as in [Add a picture](https://readitinabook.com/docs/en/how-to/add-a-picture/). Then make it a figure with a caption, by adding `{.figure title="…"}` straight after it. The title is the caption shown under the photograph, and the description in square brackets is for readers who cannot see it.

``` djot
![A studio portrait of a seated man with spectacles and a moustache, and a young woman in a dark flowered dress standing beside him with her hand in his.](../Images/kerr_thomas_margaret_1890.jpg){.figure title="Thomas and Margaret Kerr, about 1890"}
```

Leave one blank line after the photograph, then add one `:region:` line for each face, one after another, with no blank lines between them. The caption then names the people in the photograph, each linked to their chapter.

![The editor with the photograph's figure line, a blank line, and two region lines, for Thomas and Margaret. The preview shows the photograph with its caption, and the names Thomas and Margaret, each linked to their chapter.](../Images/screen-family-photo.png){.figure .screenshot}

Readers can explore the photograph from its caption, where each name lights up its face. If anything comes between the photograph and its regions, the regions appear as plain text instead.

### Mark the faces in the Photo Regions panel

1. Put the cursor on the line after the blank line below the photograph.
2. Choose [Photo Regions]{.ui} from the [Insert]{.ui} list above the editor. The panel opens with the chapter's photograph.
3. Drag a box around each face. Each box gets a number, and a row for it appears below the photograph.
4. In each row, type the person's id, or a name for someone with no record. Add the name to show in the caption, and a row such as "Standing" or "Seated" to group the names.
5. Click [Insert]{.ui}, and the panel writes the region lines.

![The Photo Regions panel with the family photograph of about 1910. Seven faces have boxes around them, and the six with a person's id are numbered. Below the photograph, one row for each box gives the person's id, the name to show and the row: George Bell, Jessie and Ewen standing, Isabella, Margaret and Thomas seated, and Annie in front. The Clear and Insert buttons are at the bottom.](../Images/screen-family-regions-panel.png){.figure .screenshot width=612}

The panel keeps the boxes. To change them later, open the panel again, change the boxes, then delete the old region lines and click [Insert]{.ui}.

The young man standing at the left of that photograph is named only on the back of the print, as George Bell. He has no record, so his region gives only a name.

### What a region line says

You can also write or correct region lines yourself. In each region:

- `at` is the box around the face, in percent of the picture: the distance from the left edge, the distance from the top, then the width and the height.
- `of` is the person's id. For someone with no record, leave it out and give only their name with `as`.
- `as` is the name shown in the caption.
- `row` groups the names, for example `row="Standing"` and `row="Seated"` in a group photograph.

## Gather portraits

`:portraits:` shows every face that has been named for a person, cropped from all the photographs in the book. Each portrait links to the photograph it came from.

![The editor with a portraits line for Thomas Kerr. The preview shows two portraits of Thomas side by side: one from the studio portrait of about 1890, and one from the family group of about 1910.](../Images/screen-family-portraits.png){.figure .screenshot}

A photograph's faces reach the portraits once the photograph's own chapter has been shown in the preview. If a portrait is missing, open the chapter that holds the photograph, then come back.

## Keep research notes

Put your research notes in chapters at the back of the book, in the reading order. Some reading apps open chapters outside the reading order in a separate window.

To link to one section of the notes, put an id on the line above its heading:

```
{#tailoring-firm}
## Ewen McInnes's tailoring firm
```

Then link to it from any chapter as `[the research notes](research.xhtml#tailoring-firm)`. An id you write yourself stays the same when you reword the heading.

For a short citation, use a footnote. Write `[^firm]` where the note belongs, and the note itself on a line of its own at the end of the chapter: `[^firm]: Sands & McDougall's directories, 1875–1885.` Keep each note to a sentence or two, and link to the research notes for more.

## Find problems in the records

Problems with the records, such as a misspelled id or a record in the wrong shape, are listed at the top of every family panel. They appear in the preview as you type, and the editor lists them below the text. Thomas Kerr's record here has a list of partners and a separate `married` line.

![The editor with Thomas Kerr's record, in which a marriage date sits beside a list of partners. The preview shows a warning above his family panel that explains the mistake, and the editor lists the same warning below the text.](../Images/screen-family-finding.png){.figure .screenshot}

## Before you share the book

Family history books often name living relatives. Ask them before you share a book that includes them.

## Record lines

| Line | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `display` | The person's name as it should appear |
| `nickname` | A name they were known by, shown after their name in the index |
| `birth`, `death` | Dates |
| `birthplace`, `deathplace` | Places |
| `parents` | The ids of the person's parents, in square brackets |
| `partner` | The id of the person they married, or a list of marriages |
| `married`, `married_place` | The date and place of a single marriage |
| `others` | Records of people who have no chapter |

## Markers

| Marker | What it shows | Settings |
|---|---|---|
| `:family:` | The person's dates, parents, brothers and sisters, and partners | `of=` |
| `:tree:` | The person's descendants | `of=`, `depth=`, `to=` with `via=` |
| `:lifeline:` | The person's life along a line of years | `of=`, `as=` |
| `:portraits:` | Every named face of the person | `of=` |
| `:family-index:` | Everyone in the records, by surname | |
| `:region:` | One face in the photograph above it | `at=`, `of=`, `as=`, `row=` |
